meta {
  name: Presign New Business Application
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/sold-deals/documents/presign
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "leadId": "{{createdLeadId}}",
    "kind": "new_business_application",
    "filename": "new-business-application.pdf",
    "contentType": "application/pdf",
    "size": 2048
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.getStatus() === 201) {
    const body = res.getBody();
    bru.setVar("nbaDocumentKey", body.key);
    bru.setVar("nbaDocumentUploadUrl", body.uploadUrl);
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 201 Created", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("signs the key under the /nba/ prefix", function () {
    // The prefix is the enforcement mechanism, not decoration — see the docs.
    expect(res.getBody().key).to.contain("/nba/");
  });

  test("still scopes the key to the agency and lead", function () {
    const key = res.getBody().key;
    expect(key).to.contain("agencies/");
    expect(key).to.contain(bru.getVar("createdLeadId"));
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /sold-deals/documents/presign — the new business application (PAC-56 #23)

  The same endpoint as `Presign Sold Document`, with `kind:
  "new_business_application"`. Two things change.

  - **Module:** `deal_audits` · **Permission:** `deal_audits:write`

  ## 1. PDF only
  The deliberate exception to the sold form's PDF-or-image rule (#22). This is a
  **signed application**, not a photographed receipt, and the data-accuracy case
  for requiring it is why David asked for it at all. An `image/png` here is a
  400.

  ## 2. Its own key prefix — and that is the real gate
  The key comes back under `agencies/<agencyId>/sold-deals/<leadId>/nba/…`
  rather than the shared prefix.

  That is not cosmetic. `assertKeyOwnership` at submit time matches the key
  against the purpose for the slot it was declared in, so a caller who presigns
  a PNG as a `discount_proof` and then declares that key as the new business
  application is rejected — **the key is under the wrong prefix**. The
  `HeadObject` content-type re-check catches the same attempt independently.
  The narrowing on *this* request is only a fast-fail; those two are what hold.

  ⚠ The `discount_proof` prefix is deliberately **unchanged** — there are
  in-flight keys in every environment and an e2e case pins it.

  ## `kind`
  | value | accepts | key prefix |
  |---|---|---|
  | `discount_proof` *(default)* | PDF, JPEG, PNG | `sold-deals/<leadId>` |
  | `new_business_application` | **PDF only** | `sold-deals/<leadId>/nba` |

  Defaulted, so every caller written before #23 keeps working untouched.

  ## Response `201`
  ```json
  {
    "key": "agencies/665f…/sold-deals/665f…/nba/2026/9f2c-new-business-application.pdf",
    "uploadUrl": "http://localhost:9000/sfa-uploads/…?X-Amz-Signature=…",
    "requiredHeaders": { "Content-Type": "application/pdf" },
    "expiresIn": 900
  }
  ```

  ## Errors
  - `400` — a non-PDF `contentType` for this kind, a malformed `leadId`, or a
    declared size over 10 MB.
  - `404` — unknown lead, or one outside the caller's data scope. Checked
    **before** anything is signed, so a presign cannot probe for leads.

  ## Run order
  Needs `createdLeadId`. Feeds `Upload New Business Application` (seq 12).

  ⚠ **10 MB ceiling.** A scanned, signed application can exceed it, and the
  failure surfaces here with a generic message. Flagged for the product owner
  rather than raised unilaterally.
}
